# What a set of documents puts in each class of each classification, and which classes it leaves
# empty. One row per (relation, class) of every classification whose rows are filed, with the
# count over whichever documents the caller chose.
#
# The scope is a slot
#   That is what this file is for. A census counts, so the set of documents it counts over is part
#   of its result, and choosing that set is the decision most easily forgotten: forget it and a
#   report about the world quietly counts the stipulations written to exercise it. A slot cannot be
#   forgotten, because a caller that supplies no `@scope` does not compose at all.
#
# Two callers, two questions
#   Over the corpus, an empty class reads as a state nobody has filed yet: a finding about the
#   evidence, and a claim about where the next document is worth having. Over the fixtures, it
#   reads as a state this repository has never had to make up, which is a claim about coverage.
#   `assets/fixtures/README.md` says why the two are never added together to fill a class.
#
# Not the contract's reading
#   epistemics/classes.sqlc counts the same cells over every document together, and is what
#   folds/class_subjects.sqlc holds the declared standings against, because whether anything
#   produces a class at all does not depend on which documents are in view. A class exercised there
#   and empty here is the ordinary case, not a disagreement: it is exactly what this report exists
#   to show.
#
# Only the filed classifications
#   `epistemics/class_sets.sqlc` declares which relations classify rows a document filed. The other
#   two classify the repository's own rules, whose classes are the same under every scope; printing
#   them here would put a zero beside a real one, and invite it to be read as a gap in the evidence.
#
# The scope is joined to each counted row, not applied after the count
#   The `LEFT JOIN` keeps every declared class whether or not any of its rows are in scope, so a
#   class emptied by the restriction is a row with zero rather than a row that vanished. An inner
#   join here would silently drop exactly the classes this report exists to name.
-- epistemics/class_cells.sqlc counted per class, restricted by the caller's @scope.
SELECT k.relation, k.class, s.subject, count(w.filing) AS balls, k.ord
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/class_cells.sqlc)
) k
JOIN      (
    :compose(epistemics/class_sets.sqlc)
) s ON s.relation = k.relation AND s.filed
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(@scope)
) w ON w.filing = k.filing
GROUP BY k.relation, k.class, s.subject, k.ord
ORDER BY k.relation, k.ord
